WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:06.586
For a long time, Marius had a fever, which made him delirious and call out Cosette's name.

2
00:00:06.587 --> 00:00:12.714
Every day a very well-dressed gentleman with white hair came to inquire after the wounded man.

3
00:00:12.715 --> 00:00:17.981
At last in September, the doctor declared Marius out of danger.

4
00:00:17.982 --> 00:00:23.778
It had been three months since he was carried from the barricade to his grandfather's house.

5
00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:27.662
Marius still had two more months of recovery ahead of him.

6
00:00:27.663 --> 00:00:34.683
Monsieur Gillenormand, relieved that Marius was alive, watched over him with great tenderness.

7
00:00:34.684 --> 00:00:40.770
As for Marius, he had one obsession: to find and marry Cosette.

8
00:00:40.771 --> 00:00:44.935
He had not said her name aloud since he recovered from his fever.

9
00:00:44.936 --> 00:00:50.175
He was not won over by his grandfather's constant concern and attention.

10
00:00:50.176 --> 00:00:58.913
"He's being nice to me now," thought Marius. "But as soon as I get well and mention Cosette, his old self will return."

11
00:00:58.914 --> 00:01:05.047
Marius was sure that Monsieur Gillenormand would once more object to his desire to marry Cosette.

12
00:01:05.048 --> 00:01:08.080
So Marius prepared to do battle.

13
00:01:08.081 --> 00:01:13.921
One day Monsieur Gillenormand made a comment about the fish that Marius was eating.

14
00:01:13.922 --> 00:01:19.414
Marius, who was looking for an argument, clenched his fists in anger.

15
00:01:19.415 --> 00:01:24.397
"This reminds me of something I've been waiting to say. I want to marry Cosette!"

16
00:01:24.398 --> 00:01:36.722
Monsieur Gillenormand laughed. "I don't see the connection between fish and Cosette, but of course you will marry her."

17
00:01:36.723 --> 00:01:41.143
Stunned, Marius trembled with happiness.

18
00:01:41.144 --> 00:01:46.699
"I know you expected an argument from me," continued Gillenormand.

19
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:57.691
"But while you've been recuperating, I've been investigating her. What a lovely young woman! I insist that you marry her!"

20
00:01:57.692 --> 00:02:03.461
Then Gillenormand embraced Marius, and they both wept with happiness.

21
00:02:03.462 --> 00:02:07.900
"Now that I'm almost well, Father, don't you think I could see her?"

22
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:12.286
"Since you called me ‘Father,' you shall see her today!"

23
00:02:12.287 --> 00:02:17.240
Later that day Cosette arrived on the doorstep of Marius' room.

24
00:02:17.241 --> 00:02:24.429
Monsieur Gillenormand, Aunt Gillenormand, and several servants were gathered at Marius' bedside.

25
00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:28.508
"Angel!" cried Marius when he saw Cosette.

26
00:02:28.509 --> 00:02:35.675
Cosette wanted to throw herself into Marius' arms but didn't dare to do that in front of so many people.

27
00:02:35.676 --> 00:02:42.067
A well-dressed man with white hair had entered the room behind Cosette. He had a sad smile.

28
00:02:42.068 --> 00:02:48.238
This was Jean Valjean, or Monsieur Fauchelevent as he was still known.

29
00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:53.500
Under his arm he was carrying a rectangular package wrapped in paper.

30
00:02:53.501 --> 00:02:59.746
"Does this man bring books wherever he goes?" Aunt Gillenormand whispered to a servant.

31
00:02:59.747 --> 00:03:05.317
"I believe he's a scholar," said Monsieur Gillenormand, who had overheard her.

32
00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:07.756
Then he bowed to Valjean.

33
00:03:07.756 --> 00:03:19.063
"Monsieur Fauchelevent, on behalf of my grandson, Baron Marius Pontmercy, I have the honor to ask for the hand of mademoiselle."

34
00:03:19.064 --> 00:03:21.984
Valjean bowed in agreement.

35
00:03:21.985 --> 00:03:27.786
Gillenormand turned toward Marius and Cosette, and extended his hands in blessing.

36
00:03:27.787 --> 00:03:36.635
"You have permission to adore each other! Now, the rest of you, talk loudly so these two can have some privacy."

37
00:03:36.636 --> 00:03:41.816
Everyone in the room burst into applause and began to talk at once.

38
00:03:41.817 --> 00:03:45.690
"I can't believe it's you!" whispered Marius.

39
00:03:45.691 --> 00:03:52.357
"Why did you go away to fight? I was so worried about you," said Cosette.

40
00:03:52.358 --> 00:03:56.155
"When I heard that we were coming here, I dashed out of the house.

41
00:03:56.156 --> 00:04:02.261
What must your family think of me? My collar is wrinkled. We're still living at 7 Rue . . ."

42
00:04:02.262 --> 00:04:07.269
She babbled on and on, completely happy at being with Marius again.

43
00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:12.340
"What a lovely daughter you have!" Gillenormand said to Valjean.

44
00:04:12.341 --> 00:04:21.102
"She is like something out of a painting. It's a shame she'll have to live very frugally because I don't have much money to give them."

45
00:04:21.103 --> 00:04:30.229
"Cosette has 600,000 francs." These were the first words that Valjean had uttered, and the room went silent.

46
00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:35.058
He laid on the table the package that Aunt Gillenormand had mistaken for a book.

47
00:04:35.059 --> 00:04:39.483
When he removed the wrapper, he revealed a stack of bank notes.

48
00:04:39.484 --> 00:04:45.562
"Actually it's closer to 584,000 francs," said Valjean.

49
00:04:45.563 --> 00:04:51.158
"Some of the money was spent on her education and household expenses."

50
00:04:51.159 --> 00:04:58.496
As for Marius and Cosette, they were so busy looking at each other that they paid little attention to this incident.

51
00:04:58.497 --> 00:05:05.845
Where had this money come from? Valjean had made a lot of money from the factory he ran as Monsieur Madeleine.

52
00:05:05.846 --> 00:05:11.502
After the Champmathieu trial, he had withdrawn 600,000 francs from the bank.

53
00:05:11.503 --> 00:05:16.011
This was the money that he buried in the woods near the Thenardiers' inn.

54
00:05:16.012 --> 00:05:21.239
Whenever he needed money, he would disappear for a few days to dig up some cash.

55
00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:26.733
He had also buried his other treasure—the bishop's silver candlesticks.

56
00:05:26.734 --> 00:05:32.979
When he read of Javert's death in the newspaper, he put the candlesticks on his mantel.

57
00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:37.730
Preparations were made for the wedding, which was to take place in February.

58
00:05:37.731 --> 00:05:44.225
Valjean did not want anything in his own or Cosette's history to prevent the marriage.

59
00:05:44.226 --> 00:05:51.638
So he invented a family for her and persuaded the nuns at the convent to sign the necessary certificates.

60
00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:57.655
At any other time, Cosette would have been upset to learn that Valjean was not her real father.

61
00:05:57.656 --> 00:06:01.632
But her happiness with Marius made those thoughts disappear.

62
00:06:01.633 --> 00:06:05.632
"I will always call you ‘Father,'" she told him.

63
00:06:05.633 --> 00:06:14.854
Cosette and Valjean came to visit Marius every day. The young man was healing well and now determined to find Thenardier.

64
00:06:14.855 --> 00:06:22.616
Marius knew Thenardier was a villain, but he still thought kindly of the rogue for saving his father's life.

65
00:06:22.617 --> 00:06:30.441
Marius also wondered about someone else—the man who had rescued him and brought him home to his grandfather.

66
00:06:30.442 --> 00:06:38.108
Marius seemed to recall having seen Fauchelevent at the barricade. He wondered if this were possible.

67
00:06:38.109 --> 00:06:43.600
"Are you familiar with the Rue de la Chanvrerie?" Marius asked Valjean one day.

68
00:06:43.601 --> 00:06:48.410
"I don't know anything about that street," replied Valjean.

69
00:06:48.411 --> 00:06:56.179
"I must have been dreaming," Marius said to himself. "I must have seen someone else who looked like Monsieur Fauchelevent."

70
00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:01.671
Marius hired people to search for these two men, but they were unsuccessful.

71
00:07:01.672 --> 00:07:11.242
At last the wedding day came. Cosette wore a beautiful white dress and a veil held in place with a wreath of orange blossoms.

72
00:07:11.243 --> 00:07:20.711
Marius looked handsome, and his grandfather beamed with pride. Valjean stood off to the side, his arm in a sling.

73
00:07:20.712 --> 00:07:25.165
After the wedding the guests went to Monsieur Gillenormand's for a banquet.

74
00:07:25.166 --> 00:07:29.140
"Where is my father?" asked Cosette as they sat down.

75
00:07:29.141 --> 00:07:35.290
"He asked me to say that his arm was bothering him and he went home," said a servant.

76
00:07:35.291 --> 00:07:42.150
In fact Valjean's arm was fine. He had bandaged it to avoid signing any papers connected with the wedding.

77
00:07:42.151 --> 00:07:47.536
As a former convict, he was not allowed to sign any legal documents.

78
00:07:47.537 --> 00:07:55.052
At home Valjean walked into Cosette's room. It was bare except for a few pieces of furniture.

79
00:07:55.053 --> 00:08:00.957
Her belongings had been taken away to the Gillenormands' house, where the couple would live.

80
00:08:00.958 --> 00:08:06.441
In Valjean's own room, he opened the small suitcase that he had always kept.

81
00:08:06.442 --> 00:08:11.774
He took out the mourning clothes that he had given to Cosette at the Thenardiers' inn.

82
00:08:11.775 --> 00:08:19.884
"She had that enormous doll," he remembered as he laid the clothes on his bed. "She took my hand and laughed.

83
00:08:19.885 --> 00:08:23.257
She had nobody but me in the world."

84
00:08:23.258 --> 00:08:27.973
Jean Valjean put his head down and sobbed.

